[node name="BriefingText" type="RichTextLabel" parent="CanvasLayer/RootUI/CenterCard/VBox"]
layout_mode = 2
size_flags_vertical = 3
bbcode_enabled = true
text = "[color=#f87171][b]SITUATION ASSESSMENT (OCTOBER 2038):[/b][/color]
Extreme Active Region AR-4188 has rotated into direct Sun-Earth alignment. Coronal imaging confirms the onset of an unprecedented coronal mass ejection (CME) shock front traveling toward Earth in excess of 2,400 km/s.

[color=#38bdf8][b]OPERATIONAL MISSION OBJECTIVE:[/b][/color]
A specialized four-officer team is authorized to construct, launch, navigate, and commission the [b]HELIOS-4[/b] early-warning spacecraft into a stable [b]halo orbit at Sun-Earth L1[/b] (~1.5 million kilometers sunward from Earth).

[color=#fbbf24][b]KEY SCIENTIFIC & ENGINEERING PRINCIPLES:[/b][/color]
• [b]Sun-Earth L1 Location:[/b] L1 is located approximately 1.5 million km sunward (only ~1% of the distance from Earth to the Sun), [b]NOT[/b] the midpoint.
• [b]Halo Orbit Dynamics:[/b] The spacecraft enters a periodic 3D halo orbit around the unstable L1 libration point. It does not stop at a fixed point, and requires continuous stationkeeping propellant.
• [b]Uncrewed Vehicle:[/b] HELIOS-4 is an autonomous robotic probe. There is no human crew, life-support, or oxygen subsystem.
• [b]NASA DSCOVR Heritage:[/b] Utilizing Faraday Cup plasma analyzers and triaxial fluxgate magnetometers to measure solar wind velocity, density, and magnetic field direction (Bz) to beam early warnings (15–60 min lead time) to Earth."

